{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793653156,"nonce":"/sLtTd0ZijkJJ7fsnwdj5xdr/vo=","title":"Plan an optional macro-component library built on blinc_cn","message":"Owner request, 2026-11-02: plan a Blinc macro-component library inspired by https://www.affectui.com/components, built on existing blinc_cn components, allowing reusable atom improvements in blinc_cn. This is separate from the 0.6.0 release goal. This task records the plan only; no crate/manifests/source changes, builds, commits or publication were performed. Existing production edits belong to the ongoing CN work.\n\nRecommended name: blinc_blocks. Macro components mean reusable workflow-sized UI compositions; blinc_macros remains the existing procedural-macro crate. Adopt original Blinc/CN styling and behavior contracts, using the reference catalog to identify useful interaction patterns. The reference splits reusable components from larger blocks/screens; the Blinc catalog should similarly compose blocks into optional example workspaces.\n\nArchitecture and boundaries\n- Add a separate optional crate above blinc_cn, using blinc_layout/core/theme for existing binding, layout, identity and theme APIs. Production components implement ElementBuilder and compose CN atoms. blinc_app and blinc_recorder belong in demos/tests, with no application runner, GPU dispatcher or transport embedded in the component library.\n- Organize modules into activity/workflows, chat, settings and insights. Consider optional family features after the first vertical slices establish actual dependency/build costs. Keep this crate out of the current curated release publish cohort until it is intentionally ready.\n- blocks_bundle() should build on cn_bundle() and append BLOCKS_STYLES. Use a distinct class namespace, inherited theme tokens, attributes/classes for visual state, and CSS transitions/keyframes or declarative motion() for motion. Include a composable stylesheet constant for custom theme bundles. No separate palette/motion engine or runtime global stylesheet registration per mounted widget.\n- Runtime props take IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e and bind in place. List membership/order uses keyed regions; retained rows hold per-item Signals/Computeds so status/text changes do not depend on re-running the row constructor. Retain stable domain IDs, use existing InstanceKey/tree scopes for widget identity, and dispose subscriptions on removal. Never use list position or labels as identity.\n- Business data is controlled by the application. Prefer typed IDs and enums, payload slots/renderers and typed intent callbacks. Components emit submit/retry/cancel/approve/reorder/save events; application services own networking, execution, persistence, permissions and operation completion. An approval click remains distinct from a confirmed successful operation. A request/revision ID prevents completion for an old request updating a newly mounted one.\n- Keep transient presentation signals local (expanded sections, hover/focus); expose handles when applications need to control them. Accept title/body/preview/actions/row slots so users can extend compositions without replacing CN styling or rebuilding the workflow.\n- Existing text editing handles remain usable by composer widgets. Signal, Computed and Effect are the new-code vocabulary. Do not invent a new state system or require a Rust macro DSL to use the crate.\n\nFirst catalog and CN reuse\n1. ToolCallCard / ToolCallGroup: card, collapsible, badge, spinner, buttons, typography and the existing layout code viewer. Running/success/error/cancelled presentation, bounded arguments/results, copy/retry intent, optional elapsed time from application-supplied times. The group is the first reusable activity-log composition.\n2. ApprovalCard: card, alert, checkbox, button, dialog, kbd and progress. Pending/submitting/approved/denied/expired/error presentation, custom preview and details slots, keyboard actions, application-owned decision and expiry handling.\n3. IntegrationSettings: card, input, select/combobox, switch, tooltip, alert, button and toast. Connection summary, configuration fields, dirty/save/cancel/error behavior and application validation. This provides a non-AI vertical slice and exercises reusable field support.\n4. ChatComposer: textarea, combobox, menus, buttons, popover, badge and tooltip. Draft handle, selected model, busy/disabled, attachments and submit/cancel intents. Define Enter versus Shift+Enter, IME composition and paste behavior, auto-growth limits, and focus preservation before implementation. Voice/file-picker integrations are caller-supplied capabilities.\n5. ConversationView: scroll area, avatar, card, rich text/code, skeleton and action buttons, with slots for ToolCallCard and ApprovalCard. Streaming updates patch the active message; preserve selection and scroll position, follow the tail only while the reader is at the tail, and support loading earlier history. Citations/source previews and message actions can live as chat subcomponents first.\n6. TaskInbox: tabs, input, badge, table/list, pagination and dropdown menu. Filtering, selection, row actions and details slots, updating rows without losing focus or expansion.\n7. PlanEditor / RunSteps: list/accordion/tree, progress, checkbox, menus and buttons. Stable step IDs, draft editing, keyboard reorder, execution progress and retry intent. Pointer reorder uses shared atom/engine support with an equivalent keyboard operation.\n8. MetricsPanel / SetupGuide: existing CN charts, card, tabs/select, progress and collapsible. Reuse the newly landed chart palette and data bindings. Keep chart rendering/animation inside the chart component. These become building blocks for an example metrics dashboard and onboarding screen.\n\nPromote generic missing atoms into blinc_cn, based on actual reuse\n- First candidates: StatusIndicator, IconButton/ActionGroup, KeyValueList, EmptyState, and shared Field/FieldGroup helpers. Check existing variants before adding a new component. Extend current inputs/textarea for reusable submit policy, bindable supporting text and sizing instead of implementing parallel editors in blocks.\n- Chat/inbox candidates: removable Chip/ChipGroup, searchable Command menu, multi-select support and a styled virtual-list facade. Extend existing combobox/dropdown/virtual_list machinery rather than duplicating navigation, focus, popup or row ownership logic.\n- Later candidates: Dropzone/AttachmentItem, Stepper/Timeline and generic reorderable list. Platform file-dialog and upload behavior are supplied by applications.\n- Keep Message, ToolCall, Approval, ModelPicker, IntegrationSettings and agent workflow schemas in blinc_blocks. Promote an atom after it is generic and needed by at least two compositions, except a proven missing foundational input/focus primitive.\n\nDelivery sequence\nA. Freeze contracts against the settled CN baseline: small API sketches, ownership/binding/slot conventions, stylesheet bundle, snapshot harness, and acceptance checklist. Audit names against current code because the old crate overview and some book pages are stale.\nB. Implement ToolCallCard, ApprovalCard and IntegrationSettings, extracting only the shared atoms they demonstrate. Deliver examples showing real caller-controlled updates rather than timer-simulated business behavior.\nC. Implement ChatComposer and ConversationView together, including scrolling/streaming/editing stress tests. Confirm the keyed virtual-list API can preserve identity under insertion/reorder and variable message heights before using it for unbounded history; the current layout VirtualList is indexed/windowed and is not yet proven as that adapter.\nD. Add TaskInbox, PlanEditor/RunSteps, MetricsPanel and SetupGuide. Compose optional agent-console, integration-center and metrics-dashboard examples from these blocks.\nE. Separate advanced projects: trace waterfall, agent graph, Kanban board, editable data grid and inline diff/review. The current CN table is a styled row/cell wrapper, so spreadsheet navigation, range selection, editing, undo and virtualization require a dedicated reusable grid design before a data-workspace block. Do not inflate the first cohort to cover every reference component.\n\nDependencies on current CN/engine work\nEach block depends on the relevant fixes below; leaf cards can progress independently of list, chart or advanced platform-semantic work.\n- 530cb3ae746f1cc9282b0683fa601f2433a30e8a394f6462ec73b341f37ee5d1 is closed (titles/descriptions bind); base new APIs on the landed reactive/CSS direction.\n- 299c8c761440e78b2167232f0b3468764dcb5c4c74adb412973609f703e165d4: field placeholder/description/error binding, currently in progress in the shared worktree. Consume the finished APIs.\n- f71137b646716cb22a347cb10ee5be3d0739e60d9bdad05c4beae26f77d2a06d: ordering and subscription loss around region departure and bound writes; affects live/reordered/animated lists.\n- 08f9941e60f97b301cb164e5ddf703520c67fddce84eb062fecabd404454c839: specificity/source-order behavior for styled region rows.\n- 3ffa360f5fb62ecf24a5536846708ebfa7c3b412ab11cf6c9c2c249591780189: bound render writes and CSS transitions/rule matching.\n- f86f698dac1f0bef3cc2307f0cf9ca7035206cffedd88806868bf1a4fbd3be95: attribute/class rule changes do not apply display. Structural conditional content should use existing show/region APIs; do not hide an engine defect with ad hoc Rust visual-state branches.\n- 969d5aa0615408ea8c69f399305627cbaf5d1f2de3ffc7d3083bb1f1ffab6eea, dac4f510863799dd3bbad9907ad3a0da67f965af5f625cf2e08995feea491a9a and 547d4356e98f1a160e1ccef3a4778b557279bf341e1a931b2fceb3ea9a6f504d: chart canvas ordering and path antialiasing. Inspect their state before insights work.\n- 57c4c44b30e5ee203455e03d6e165788256e3c7c627b321eaed83c17aec5ed1a: book examples reference absent APIs. New snippets should be compile-checked instead of inheriting old examples blindly.\n- Audit reusable focus/keyboard semantics, platform accessibility role/name/announcement plumbing and reduced-motion capability. Current toggle aria_label is a no-op and web docs list accessibility pending, so existing CN naming/docs are not evidence of complete screen-reader support. Implement underlying missing semantic capabilities at the engine/atom layer and publish an honest support matrix. Audit container-responsive layout support rather than transplanting web container-query CSS into native layouts.\n\nAcceptance and performance\n- Each component: documented props/slots/events, a small state/transition table, keyboard and pointer behavior, constrained-width/long-content layout, light/dark/theme-shape coverage, disabled/loading/empty/error/retry cases, and focus restoration through overlays and row changes. Verify actual platform semantics before making accessibility claims; cover reduced-motion behavior when the substrate exists.\n- Meaningful tests: event payloads and lifecycle, no duplicated callback on repeated updates, stale completion isolation, pending/cancel/retry paths, mount/unmount cleanup, and stable IDs/focus/selection across reorder/filter/removal. Recorder snapshots should be rendered and visually inspected. Check CN atom parity instead of snapshotting only the happy-path composition.\n- Streaming lists patch active leaves, coalesce updates to frame cadence, avoid cloning full histories, and cap/virtualize expensive text/code payloads. Shared/coarse visible-only clocks drive elapsed labels; idle/hidden/settled UI requests no continuous frames. Measure update cost, allocations and retained resources with bounded and long histories before publishing performance claims.\n- Validation remains scoped to the selected block and any extracted CN atom, with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 for Cargo. No builds run for this planning task. No release schedule or timing estimate is implied; sequencing is based on dependencies and reusable value.\n\nProposed fluent shape, not an existing API:\nblocks::chat_composer(\u0026draft)\n    .key(\"support-reply\")\n    .models(models)\n    .selected_model(selected_model)\n    .busy(is_sending)\n    .on_submit(send_message)\n\nmodels and presentation variant are structural configuration; selected_model and busy accept reactive values; draft uses the existing editor handle. on_submit receives a typed snapshot (text, model ID, attachment IDs). Exact signatures should be decided after CN's in-progress editor/binding work lands.\n\nReference pages reviewed on 2026-11-02:\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components\nhttps://www.affectui.com/blocks\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components/chat-composer\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components/tool-call-card\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components/approval-card\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components/agent-plan\nhttps://www.affectui.com/components/data-table\n\nLocal source anchors: blinc_cn/src/components/mod.rs, theme.rs and cn_styles.rs; components/{table,textarea,combobox,chart/*}; blinc_layout/src/{div,region,key}.rs and widgets/virtual_list.rs. Review was read-only while the CN working tree continued changing; tracker statuses and API details must be rechecked before implementation.","files":null}]}